# Clean Code: Comments

## Explain yourself in code, not comments

```php
// BAD
// Check if user is eligible for discount
if ($user->age >= 65 || $user->membership_years > 5) { ... }

// GOOD
if ($user->isEligibleForDiscount()) { ... }
```

## Don't be redundant

```php
// BAD
/** Returns the user's name */
public function getName(): string { return $this->name; }

// GOOD - no comment needed, code is self-explanatory
public function getName(): string { return $this->name; }
```

## Don't add obvious noise

```php
// BAD
$total = 0; // set total to zero

// GOOD
$total = 0;
```

## Don't use closing brace comments

```php
// BAD
if ($isActive) {
    // ...
} // end if isActive

// GOOD
if ($isActive) {
    // ...
}
```

## Don't comment out code - remove it (version control exists)

```php
// BAD
// $user->sendNotification();
// $logger->info('Notification sent');
$user->save();

// GOOD
$user->save();
```

## Use comments to explain intent (WHY, not WHAT)

```php
// BAD
$retryLimit = 3; // set retry limit to 3

// GOOD
// Payment gateway recommends max 3 retries to avoid duplicate charges
$retryLimit = 3;
```

## Use comments for non-obvious clarification

```php
// GOOD - clarifies a non-obvious regex
// Matches Indonesian phone: +62 or 08, followed by 8-13 digits
$pattern = '/^(\+62|62|0)8[1-9][0-9]{7,11}$/';
```

## Use comments as warning of consequences

```php
// GOOD - warns about impact
// WARNING: This migration drops the legacy_users table permanently
Schema::dropIfExists('legacy_users');
```
